A||N||9||Heisenhund||Various Brake upgrades||||5||
Z||000000||Heisenhund||06-12-2000||05:15 AM||||Hello all,<P>I've read about brake upgrades using mid 80's Grand Am brakes (at least on the rear). The drawback being that the e-brake becomes useless. Since my job requires occasional transfers to other states for a couple years at a time, this option isn't really appealing.<P>However, I've recently read elsewhere that the brakes from mid 90's Sunbirds work as well and allow you to keep the e-brake.<P>Anyone know about this kind of stuff?<P>Heis<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fret Naught||06-13-2000||01:12 AM||||Thought I would give you my two cents on brakes.  I find that mine are very functional, I live in a mountainous area, and have developed the habit of not using the brake very much, my first couple of years here I went through three sets of them (not on a Fiero) just using them too much. I understand that Fieros are notorious for brake fade when heated up.  A friend of mine at work just did a brake upgrade on his Ford explored because he tows a boat all the time, he purchased slotted rotors, not cross drilled. He also got the pads they recommended. He says the difference in his braking is like night and day. The cost? 100.00 per wheel. I think the next time I do a brake job I am going to go that route rather than a big change over, I don't want to lose my E Brake either. And the upgrade is simple and rather in-expensive. Have you heard of slotted rotors? up until now I never had. They say they are much better than cross drilled as the cross drill aids in stess fractures.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phoenix||06-13-2000||08:24 AM||||Slotted rotors have been around a long time. Been using them on my auto-x cars for years. They work along the same lines as drilled rotors, excess gasses are vented out between the rotor face and the pads resulting in better braking. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||06-13-2000||10:13 AM||||Since my car is a 5-speed, I want to keep the parking brake. Occasionally, I need to get out of the car (like retrieving the newspaper) while stopped on a slope. I don't want to have to turn the car off and lock it in gear to keep it from rolling.<P>To make improvements in the braking system in a Fiero, you don't need to change the rear calipers and rotors, only the front and the master cylinder. The majority of your braking force is in the front. This is where the brake fade is most apparent.<P>By upgrading the master cylinder, you will increase the braking force in the front AND back brakes. This could cause the rear brakes to lock up earlier then the front. But, by installing a adjustable regulator, you can easily balance the braking system. (See <A HREF="http://www.jegs.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.jegs.com</A>  part#950-260-2220)<P>This is the type of upgrade I'm planning on my car.<P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||06-13-2000||11:41 AM||||This is the device to which I was referring:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/9502602220_large[1].jpg"> <P>Splice this into your rear brake line and you can easily balance your system with the larger master cylinder and bigger front brakes.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000005||batboy||06-13-2000||02:33 PM||||Yep, an adjustable proportioning valve. This has been discussed in several topics in the last year. Do a search for more info.||||reg||1
Z||000006||dman||06-13-2000||03:30 PM||||Hello everyone.<P>Good suggestions, but I'd love to hear more info about Heisenhund's original query:<P>"brakes from mid 90's Sunbirds work as well and allow you to keep the e-brake"<P>Heisenhund, do you recall where you read that?<P>Does anyone know any other information about this?<P>I know you can put Camaro brake components on a Fiero. I've seen them and they are awesome, but they aren't a simple bolt on.<P>If the Sunbird brake components work, are they bolt on (or close to it) like the Grand-Am/Baretta upgrade?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Heisenhund||06-13-2000||06:47 PM||||I actually read this as a reply in another Fiero discussion group to one member's description of his brake upgrade. The first person had accomplished the Grand Am swap and was telling of the general ease & relative cheapness (cost-wise) of the operation. The reply stated that he had heard of the Sunbird swap being as easy. Unfortunately the reply gave no specifics. I'm still waiting for a reply to my question in that post.<P>Heis<BR>||||reg||2
Z||000008||lowCG||06-13-2000||08:14 PM||||Not to be a downer or anything,but I doubt that the Sunbird rear brakes are any better than the stock Fiero rear brakes.<BR>Wish I had more time to install the 4 piston BMW calipers and 10.5" Subaru Legacy rotors I've had sitting here.Will need to make some little mounting brackets,and eventually look for a small caliper to use as an e-brake.||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||06-14-2000||03:05 PM||||While I know absolutely nothing about this mid-90's Sunbird brake swap that was mentioned, I've been trying to research it a little. Although I can't seem to find 100% reliable info, it would seem that the Sunbird name was dropped after 1994. Pontiac introduced the Sunfire in 1995 to fill in the gap. Somewhere about 96 or 97, they introduced a Sunfire GT with the 2.4 double overhead cam (the new generation quad 4). The one review I could find about the Sunfire GT said it had front disc and rear drum brakes. Whether this info is accurate, I don't know. I'm more confused now than I was before.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 06-14-2000).]||||reg||1
